<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  M365-Lang-da-dk : install-M365-Lang-da-dk.xml

  Danish (Denmark) language pack, da-dk, added to an existing Microsoft 365
  Apps installation. Company Portal available install.

  Worked example estate:
    Base language is en-us. da-dk is the requestable extra. The device already
    has Microsoft 365 Apps on Monthly Enterprise Channel, 64-bit.

  da-dk is the worked example and it runs consistently through all three files
  in this folder: this XML, the uninstall XML and Detect-M365LangDaDk.ps1. To
  offer another language, copy the whole folder and change the culture code in
  all three files together. Learn's language table gives the supported culture
  codes. Danish companion proofing languages are Danish, English, German and
  Swedish.

  WHY THERE IS NO Channel AND NO OfficeClientEdition

  Same reasoning as the Visio and Project add-on packages. Per the ODT
  reference on Microsoft Learn, when Microsoft 365 Apps is already installed
  and Channel is not specified the ODT matches the channel of the existing
  installation, and the same applies to architecture when OfficeClientEdition
  is omitted. A language request must not re-channel the device's Office.

  Version="MatchInstalled" pins the language pack to the build already on the
  device. Without it, the ODT installs the latest available build, which can
  drag the whole installation forward on a schedule nobody chose, just because
  a user asked for Danish.

  Note the documented contradiction, covered in full in README.md: the ODT
  configuration reference states Channel is required alongside
  Version="MatchInstalled", while the Microsoft 365 Apps Rangers best practices
  page gives a language pack config as its worked example and omits Channel
  deliberately. This file follows the best practices page.

  WHY THERE IS NO TargetProduct ATTRIBUTE HERE

  This is worth reading carefully, because it contradicts a common assumption.

  TargetProduct is an attribute of the Language element, and per the ODT
  reference it is only meaningful "When using MatchInstalled", meaning when the
  Language ID itself is MatchInstalled. It answers the question "which product's
  installed languages should I copy". Here the Language ID is the literal value
  da-dk, so there is nothing to match and TargetProduct has no effect.

  The Rangers best practices page shows exactly this shape for a language pack:
  Add Version="MatchInstalled", Product ID="LanguagePack", and a literal
  Language ID. No TargetProduct.

  There is also a reason to actively avoid adding it. The ExcludeApp section of
  the ODT reference says that when a configuration file used against an
  existing install lists all the languages already on the device, its ExcludeApp
  setting OVERRIDES the previous ExcludeApp settings, and when it does not list
  them all, the two are COMBINED. Adding
  Language ID="MatchInstalled" TargetProduct="All" to this file would enumerate
  every installed language, and this file specifies no ExcludeApp elements at
  all. The override path is therefore the one that would apply, and it could
  reinstate applications the base package deliberately excluded, such as new
  Outlook. A language request must not undo an application exclusion decision.

  See MIGRATION.md, defect 3, for the full account.
-->
<Configuration ID="M365-Lang-da-dk">

  <Add Version="MatchInstalled" AllowCdnFallback="TRUE">
    <Product ID="LanguagePack">
      <Language ID="da-dk" />
    </Product>
  </Add>

  <Display Level="None" AcceptEULA="TRUE" />

  <Property Name="FORCEAPPSHUTDOWN" Value="FALSE" />

</Configuration>
